---
// Case: Flowculate. Facts from CLAUDE.md (1h), befund/about-material.md and the old project pages; the dev team
// wrote the code, Robert conceived the program, did UX research, UI, brand and project management.
// Images are Robert's own presentation boards from the old site (~/Portfolio/flowculateUX) and the corporate design.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { full } from '../../lib/widths';
import CaseLayout from '../../components/case/CaseLayout.astro';
import maths from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/maths.jpg';
import pocNotes from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/poc-notes.jpg';
import workshop from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/workshop.jpg';
import uiTests from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/ui-tests.jpg';
import components from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/components.jpg';
import tools from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/tools.jpg';
import version1 from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/version-1.jpg';
import version2 from '../../assets/cases/flowculate/version-2.jpg';

import FlowculateParts from '../../components/case/diagrams/FlowculateParts.astro';
import FlowculateIdentity from '../../components/case/diagrams/FlowculateIdentity.astro';
import FlowculateRoadmap from '../../components/case/diagrams/FlowculateRoadmap.astro';
import ScrubFigure from '../../components/stage/ScrubFigure.astro';

const widths = [480, 800, 1200, 1800];
const wide = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw';
const half = '(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 50vw';

/** The film opens the page as a stage across the width (version J, live since 27 October 2026). */
const { stage = true } = Astro.props as { stage?: boolean };
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Flowculate"
  years="2022–25"
  summary="A crowd simulation that only mathematicians could run became a web app that event planners can use."
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'UX Researcher / UI Designer: concept, UX research, UI, brand, project management'],
    ['Team', 'Mathematics lead Prof. Rebekka Axthelm, one developer on a half-time post, up to four student developers I led'],
    ['Client', 'Institute for Optical Systems, HTWG Konstanz (half-time position)'],
    ['Period', 'Oct 2022 – Sep 2025'],
    ['Status', 'Live'],
  ]}
  links={[{ href: 'https://flowculate.eu', label: 'flowculate.eu' }]}
  film="flowculate"
  stage={stage ? 'flowculate' : undefined}
  next={{ href: '/work/saunter/', title: 'Saunter' }}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">When I joined in October 2022, there was only the maths.</p>
    <p>
      The research group at HTWG Konstanz had a new continuous model for how crowds move through a building, and how an
      infection could spread among them. It calculated faster and needed less computing power than the models before.
      The model ran in MATLAB, and a floor plan had to be typed in as code. A first Java website, the proof of concept,
      showed that the simulation could run in a browser, but there was nothing a planner could open and use.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question that guided my research: how do we build one program that is precise enough for mathematicians and
      simple enough for someone who plans a lecture hall, a fair stand or an evacuation route?
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image src={maths} alt="Simulation output as the mathematicians saw it: 3D density plots and a velocity field around two obstacles" widths={full(widths, maths)} sizes={wide} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The starting point: density and velocity fields, readable for mathematicians only.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image src={pocNotes} alt="Diagram of the proof-of-concept workflow, annotated by hand in green and purple marker" widths={full(widths, pocNotes)} sizes={wide} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Working through the proof of concept, 2022: five manual steps, no evaluation, no graphics.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">I conceived the program around the simulation, then planned and steered how it was built.</p>
    <p>
      Everything except the calculation itself started with me: the split into a project website and the program,
      the three steps of the program, what a user gets out at the end, and how students, researchers and the head
      of the project work with it. What a user does first, what the maths needs as input, and how the results come
      back: I ran the user research, benchmarked competitors, mapped the requirements and designed the interface
      and the brand. The code was written by the development team.
    </p>
    <p>
      On 28 June 2023 I ran a user-testing workshop at HTWG Konstanz with the project partners, the people who would use
      and fund the tool. They tested version 1.0, and we sorted every wish by effort and timing, so that a small team
      built the things that mattered first.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image src={workshop} alt="Digital summary of the user-testing workshop: the partners' wishes as sticky notes, sorted by effort and time, most of them ticked off" widths={full(widths, workshop)} sizes={wide} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The workshop summary: the partners’ wishes, sorted by effort (up) and timing (across), ticked off as they shipped.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Three separate parts, one page per step, and a brand drawn from the maths.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Three parts instead of one.</strong> I split the project into a project website, the web application and
      the computational simulation, each with its own owner and pace. The mathematicians could change the model without
      breaking the interface, and the student developers could work on the app without touching the maths.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <FlowculateParts />
      <figcaption>Project website, web application, computational simulation: three parts, three owners.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>One page per step.</strong> Instead of one screen full of parameters, the app follows the order of the
      work: draw or import the plan, set up the scenario, run the simulation. Colour is kept for the simulation data,
      the rest of the interface stays quiet. Tooltips explain each parameter in plain words, a DXF import brings in real
      floor plans, and results go out as CSV, a PDF report or raw data.
    </p>
    {
      stage && (
        <figure>
          <ScrubFigure kind="flowculate-pages" ratio="810 / 630" />
          <figcaption>The plan from the top of the page, through the app's three pages as you scroll: plan, scenario, simulation.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      )
    }
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image src={tools} alt="Interface details: interactive graph, DXF import, tooltips, interactive module, guidelines and the density heat map in a floor plan" widths={full(widths, tools)} sizes={wide} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Tools from version 2.0: graphs, DXF import, tooltips, guidelines, the density map.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Image src={uiTests} alt="Four colour variants of the same floor-plan screen, tested side by side" widths={full(widths, uiTests)} sizes={half} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>UI tests: four variants of one screen.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image src={components} alt="Component overview on a dark background: labels, text fields, toggles, sliders, tabs and buttons in their states" widths={full(widths, components)} sizes={half} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The components, with every state.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>A brand drawn from the maths.</strong> I named the project Flowculate, from flow and calculate, and built
      the logo from the Delaunay triangulation that divides a room into the triangles the model computes on. The density
      gradients of the simulation became the colours of posters, documents and presentations.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="flowculate-name" ratio="1200 / 520" narrow="600 / 920" />
      <figcaption>Flow plus calculate: the name, the mark and the lockup, from the logo presentation of May 2023.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <FlowculateIdentity />
      <figcaption>The UX identity: six principles for one calm, precise tool.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Team</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Mathematicians, developers and partners, kept on one plan.</p>
    <p>
      Prof. Rebekka Axthelm led the mathematics and the research. The application was built by one developer on a
      half-time post and, at its peak, four student developers. I planned their work, set the priorities and wrote
      the requirements they implemented. Most people on the project had no design background, so a large part of
      my job was explaining design decisions in their terms.
    </p>
    <p>
      I left the project in September 2025. The team page on flowculate.eu lists only the current team.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Flowculate runs in the browser at flowculate.eu.</p>
    <p>
      A planner can import a floor plan, place people and exits, run the simulation and export the result without knowing
      the model behind it. The same identity carries the website, the app, the project film I made to show how the
      program works, posters for fairs and conferences, the media for the Tag der Wissenschaft at HTWG, and the project's
      documents.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Image src={version1} alt="Version 1 of the web app: floor plan with a density heat map, parameters on the left, charts on the right" widths={full(widths, version1)} sizes={half} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Version 1.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image src={version2} alt="Version 2 of the web app: a calmer layout with the floor plan in the centre and grouped parameters" widths={full(widths, version2)} sizes={half} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Version 2: the plan in the centre, the parameters grouped and quiet.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div><dt>3 yrs</dt><dd>from maths only to a public web app</dd></div>
      <div><dt>4</dt><dd>student developers at peak, led by me</dd></div>
      <div><dt>3</dt><dd>parts: website, app, simulation</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Timeline</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">From proof of concept to version 2.0 in just under three years.</p>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
      <li><time>Before 10/2022</time><span>Proof of concept and MVP show the simulation runs in a browser.</span></li>
      <li><time>10/2022</time><span>I start: user research, competitor benchmark, requirements map.</span></li>
      <li><time>01/2023</time><span>Development of version 1.0, usability and performance evaluation.</span></li>
      <li><time>06/2023</time><span>User-testing workshop with the partners, priorities for a small team.</span></li>
      <li><time>01/2024</time><span>Version 1.5; UX design for version 2.0.</span></li>
      <li><time>2024</time><span>UI design 2.0, design system and style guide.</span></li>
      <li><time>01/2025</time><span>Development of version 2.0, live at flowculate.eu.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <FlowculateRoadmap />
      <figcaption>The roadmap I kept for the project, redrawn for this page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
